#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
PA_cylindrical_reconstruction_3ring_delaunay.py
================================
Reconstruct a connected, capped 3-D pulmonary artery (PA) surface from a
*trimmed* VTK centerline (.vtp), without using an existing 3-D vessel surface.

DESIGN
------
  (1) Build a rooted, directed centerline tree from trimmed VMTK lines.
  (2) At EVERY original centerline point on a branch, create one circular
      perimeter at Radius, oriented toward the NEXT downstream point.
      The last ring points along the final upstream->downstream direction.
  (3) Append the EXACT circle points for each local group of three rings,
      e.g. (0,1,2), (1,2,3), (2,3,4). Use vtkDelaunay3D, followed by
      vtkDataSetSurfaceFilter, to form ONE local, capped 3-ring volume.
      Groups overlap to remove gaps. At branch points, daughter groups are
      separately constructed with their own downstream-oriented rings.
  (4) Rasterize the signed distance to EACH closed Delaunay hull locally,
      take the UNION of the local solids, and extract a single surface.
      There is NO global Delaunay over multiple branches.
  (5) Attach approximate inlet/outlet face IDs and extensive diagnostics.

NOTES:
- Delaunay3D fills the CONVEX HULL of each local 3-ring point cloud.
  The middle ring influences the hull only where it lies on the convex envelope;
  this is deliberate for the requested experiment, but tight bends can bulge.
- The final union is voxelized. Terminal caps are approximate and face IDs
  should be checked before CFD meshing.
- The script requires a regular Python environment (NumPy, SciPy, VTK).

Run:
  python PA_cylindrical_reconstruction_3ring_delaunay.py \
    --input /path/to/trimmed.vtp --output-dir /path/to/output

Requirements: numpy, scipy, vtk. Python 3.9+ (including 3.12).
"""

